# What must NOT reach the build context.
#
# Two separate concerns are in this file and they are not equally serious.
# Most lines are size: the Dockerfile does one `COPY . /app`, so anything
# left here is uploaded to the daemon and then baked into a layer. A few
# lines are DISCLOSURE, and those are marked -- a build context is not a
# private thing, it lands in an image that gets copied to hosts.

# --- Disclosure ------------------------------------------------------------

# `.env.local` holds this machine's NOCTORNAL_TOTP_KEK, and losing or
# leaking that key is the whole ballgame: it seals every TOTP secret at
# rest. Baking it into a production image would ship a development key to
# a server AND put the dev database DSN one `scripts/_env.py` call away --
# and `load_env_local()` only declines to override variables that are
# already set, so a variable compose forgot would silently come from here.
.env
.env.*
.env.local

# Docker reads a pattern as anchored at the context root, unlike .gitignore:
# `.env` above matched the root's .env and nothing else, so apps/api/.env
# and infra/production/.env.production were COPYed into the image (verify:g38
# and the g48 verification, 2026-10-03). `**/` matches at any depth, the root
# included. `secrets.env.bak`, `secrets.env~` and `secrets-prod.env.save`,
# which an editor or an operator leaves beside the real file, were in
# neither list: `*.env`, `*.env.*` and `*.env~` take all of them, and the
# tracked `<name>.env.example` templates are put back (the `?*` keeps a bare
# `.env.example` out, as `.env.*` always did). .gitignore carries the same
# set, and apps/api/tests/test_g48_image_context.py holds the two lists to
# each other in both directions.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.env
**/*.env.*
**/*.env~
!**/?*.env.example

# Private keys and keystores, wherever one is left. Nothing in the image
# reads a key file: the TLS keypair is bind-mounted by compose.
**/*.key
**/*.pem
**/*.p12
**/*.pfx
**/*.jks

# The copy an editor or a careful operator leaves beside a secret file: a
# backup, an editor swap file, a half-written temp file. Each holds
# everything the original held and none of them is named anywhere else. No
# tracked file ends in one of these, and .gitignore carries the same list.
**/*~
**/*.swp
**/*.swo
**/*.bak
**/*.old
**/*.orig
**/*.save
**/*.tmp

# Renders of real panes against real case data. A screenshot of an
# AMBER_STRICT case carries that case's classification and does not look
# like it does (the same reasoning as .gitignore). By name and by kind, at
# any depth: `--out` can point anywhere, and a bare `screenshots/` only
# matched the one at the root. The tracked docs/images renders are a README
# asset the image never serves.
**/screenshots/
**/shots/
**/*.png

# Machine-local tool settings. The agent's permission grants
# (.claude/settings.local.json) are the text of a command somebody approved
# once, and an editor's launch configuration carries the environment it runs
# with; either can hold a token or a DSN. .gitignore keeps them out of a
# commit and this keeps them out of an image.
**/.claude/
**/.vscode/
**/.idea/

# --- Secrets that live under infra/production ------------------------------

# The operator keeps real secrets beside the compose file: secrets.env,
# collector.env (the persona key, which the API must not hold even on disk),
# egress-proxy.env (the seal key and the proxy's DSN), egress-client.env,
# postgres-init.env (the BYPASSRLS worker role's password), migrate.env (the
# schema owner's DSN), caddy.env, the backups
# scripts/production_secrets.py takes of each (`NAME.backup-UTCSTAMP`) and
# its half-written temporary file (`.NAME.tmp-PID`), and tls/private.key (the
# object store's TLS key, which would otherwise travel in every layer to
# wherever the image goes). All are gitignored, which means nothing stops
# them being COPYed into the one image every service runs, the analysis
# worker's and the collector's included (the collector is the one service
# that holds the persona key, and the API container must not carry it on
# disk either).
#
# A list of NAMES lost that race twice (2026-10-03): the first named
# secrets.env and tls/ and missed the egress and init env files, the next
# named five env files and missed collector.env.old, secrets.env.bak,
# .env.production, private.key and signing.pem. A list of SHAPES cannot name
# a file nobody foresaw either. So the directory is excluded as a whole and
# only what is tracked and holds no secret is let back in, at the end of
# this section: the `.example` templates, which hold placeholders only, and
# the compose file. A name nobody listed, a backup, an editor copy, a stray
# key, is out until somebody decides otherwise.
# Nothing in the image reads this directory: compose, the Caddyfile and the
# README are used on the host, and compose BIND-mounts what a container needs
# (tls/ included, so nothing is lost by leaving it out). The tests that hold
# this are test_dockerignore_secrets.py (every name .gitignore keeps out, at
# every depth) and test_g48_image_context.py.
infra/production/*

# MinIO's TLS keypair, including `private.key`, named as well. Without this
# the private key of the production object store would be baked into every
# layer of the image and travel with it to wherever the image goes.
infra/production/tls/

# What is let back in: tracked, and no secret in any of it.
!infra/production/*.env.example
!infra/production/compose.yml

# Database dumps and backups (infra-2, 2026-10-03). The documented backup
# once wrote ./noctornal-<timestamp>.sql into the repository root, which is
# the build context, and the next `up -d --build` copied the whole case
# database in plaintext into every container. Root-level patterns on
# purpose: a `*.sql` that matched at every depth would drop db/init and
# db/schema.sql from the image. The documented backup goes OUTSIDE the
# checkout (infra/production/README.md); this is the net under it.
*.sql
*.sql.*
*.dump
*.dump.*
noctornal-*
backup/
backups/
# The analysis worker's image is this image (compose.yml), so these lines are also
# what lets its "holds no secret" rest on more than a file's mode (F42 verify 5).

# --- Size and staleness ----------------------------------------------------

# A host virtualenv is the wrong platform's binaries (this repo is
# developed on Windows) and would shadow nothing useful -- the image
# installs its own.
.venv/
apps/api/.venv/

.git/
.github/

# Build artefacts of a DIFFERENT distribution channel. `release/` is the
# hand-installer path (install.sh / INSTALL.md), which a container
# deployment does not use, and the packaged zip is a copy of the whole
# repository -- committing it into an image doubles the layer for nothing.
release/
dist/
*.zip

node_modules/
.next/

__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# YARA rule sources are cloned into these two directories by
# scripts/yara_db.py. `yara/sources.json` and `yara/README.md` are the
# tracked configuration and DO belong in the image; the clones do not.
yara/vendor/
yara/dist/

# Editor and agent noise.
.vscode/
.idea/
.claude/
